WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.049
With Big Hero 6 out now, let’s take a look
at 15 things you probably didn’t know about

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:07.790
Disney’s animated hit!

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:14.230
The design of Baymax’s face – two dots
joined by a line – was inspired by the look

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:21.200
of Japanese suzu bells, which director Don
Hall saw on a visit to Japan.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.400
The huggable design of healthcare robot Baymax
was inspired by director Don Hall’s visit

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.750
to Carnegie Mellon University where he spent
time with researchers working on soft robotics

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:36.820
such as an inflatable vinyl arm that could
perform simple tasks like brushing someone’s

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:37.820
teeth.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:43.350
According to lead character designer Shiyoon
Kim, the design of Baymax was also influenced

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:48.360
by Japanese infomercials which often highlight
a product’s cute design rather than its

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.760
technology.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.870
When it came to making Baymax move, the animators
looked at real robots, movie robots, babies,

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.339
and even koala bears for inspiration!

00:00:58.339 --> 00:01:03.239
But in the end, they found that the way baby
penguins moved was the best fit for Baymax.

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:07.370
The reason for this was that baby penguins
have similar body proportions to the Big Hero

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:12.940
6 robot, in other words, they’ve got long
torsos and short legs.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:18.590
When it came to bringing Baymax to life, the
key word for the Big Hero 6 artists was restraint.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:23.700
Because Baymax is a robot and has limited
movements, it was important to keep the animation

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:30.540
minimal and separate out movements, so, for
example, Baymax walks forward and then waves.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:36.650
In fact, rather than animating Baymax, the
artists referred to their work as ‘un-imating’

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:37.650
him.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.680
And as Baymax doesn’t have a mouth or human
facial expressions, the artists had to find

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.360
ways to capture quick and easily-readable
poses – for example, how much he moved his

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.140
head or blinked his eyes.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:55.220
Big Hero 6 averages more main characters on
screen at one time than in any previous movie

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.750
by Walt Disney Animation Studios!

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:03.490
When the artists behind Big Hero 6 were looking
for inspiration for chewing-gum-popping daredevil

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:08.289
GoGo Tomago, whose catchphrase is “woman
up”, they looked at actors such as John

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:13.870
Wayne, Clint Eastwood and Gary Cooper to study
their cool, emotionally reserved traits.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:21.170
They also looked at bike messengers and speed
skaters to inform GoGo’s body type and movements.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:26.549
Director Don Hall got the idea for the superhero
suit worn by laid-back comic book fan Fred

00:02:26.549 --> 00:02:31.860
when he saw online videos of people dressed
in Kaiju costumes having wrestling matches!

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:38.290
Fred’s fire-breathing super-suit includes
claws, integrated communications, and a super

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.290
bounce!

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:43.799
Hundreds of background characters populate
the world of San Fransokyo.

00:02:43.799 --> 00:02:49.230
To make the city feel believable, those characters
are more detailed and varied than ever before,

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:52.609
coming in all different shapes, sizes, cultures
and fashions.

00:02:52.609 --> 00:02:57.430
The Big Hero 6 artists used a software called
Denizen, which was created by Walt Disney

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:01.129
Animation Studios, to create and animate the
crowds.

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:07.639
With this tech the artists created 670 unique
characters, compared to 270 unique characters

00:03:07.639 --> 00:03:12.239
in Frozen, 185 in Wreck-It Ralph, and 80 in
Tangled.

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:18.939
Each of those 670 unique characters in Big
Hero 6 has up to 32 different clothing look

00:03:18.939 --> 00:03:22.510
combinations, plus 32 different hair and skin
tones.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:30.549
All of which means the Big Hero 6 filmmakers
could have 686,080 unique characters in San

00:03:30.549 --> 00:03:31.969
Fransokyo!

00:03:31.969 --> 00:03:36.450
Walt Disney Animation Studios encouraged their
employees to include themselves as background

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.310
characters in Big Hero 6.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:42.609
Which means that the movie’s crowd scenes
feature cameos from 200 Walt Disney Animation

00:03:42.609 --> 00:03:43.909
Studios employees!

00:03:43.909 --> 00:03:50.680
The city of San Fransokyo, where Big Hero
6 takes place, may be a fictional mash-up

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:55.900
of San Francisco and Tokyo, but the creation
of that fictional world began with real-world

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:56.900
geography.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.380
Not only did the filmmakers take research
trips to both San Francisco and Tokyo, but

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:06.639
the movie’s artists also used actual maps
of San Francisco, including the layout of

00:04:06.639 --> 00:04:11.299
the streets and the size of the lots, as a
blueprint to give their city an authentic

00:04:11.299 --> 00:04:12.299
look.

00:04:12.299 --> 00:04:16.949
In addition to that real data, the artists
incorporated the visual style of Tokyo’s

00:04:16.949 --> 00:04:21.959
architecture, neon lights and signage, and
stylised everything to make it unique to San

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:26.190
Fransokyo.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:32.450
San Fransokyo features over 80,000 buildings,
100,000 vehicles, over 200,000 streetlights

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:36.830
and over 250,000 trees.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:41.870
The labs at the San Fransokyo Institute of
Technology were inspired by research trips

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:48.330
the Big Hero 6 team made to several US universities
including Harvard and MIT, and also to Pasadena’s

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:51.400
Jet Propulsion Lab.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:56.560
To help make the movie’s big finale relate
to real-world research, the filmmakers consulted

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:02.889
theoretical physicist Sean Carroll, who does
research at Caltech on cosmology, field theory,

00:05:02.889 --> 00:05:06.139
gravity, and quantum mechanics!

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:10.650
Director Don Hall’s kids have been pitching
him sequel ideas for Big Hero 6 and they’d

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:15.190
particularly love to see ninja Baymax come
out of the portal to fight good Baymax.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:19.770
Well there you have it, 15 things you probably
didn't know about Big Hero 6!

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:23.099
Now what would you love to see in a Big Hero
6 sequel?

